# Cross-platform security audit

Runs read-only host checks and delegates project analysis to established
scanners. Results are normalized, grouped by severity, and matched to CISA's
Known Exploited Vulnerabilities catalog when a finding contains a CVE ID.

The command does not claim that a machine is secure or compliant. `audit` and
`doctor` never apply host-hardening changes. They show evidence and recommend
remediation for a person to review. `--refresh-kev` only refreshes the local
catalog cache.

## Actions

### `audit`

With `--scope system`, inspect native update, malware-protection, disk
encryption, firewall, application-trust, secure-boot, and mandatory-access
control status where the host exposes them.

With `--scope project`, `--scanner auto` runs each installed supported scanner:
OSV-Scanner v2 for dependency vulnerabilities, Trivy for filesystem
vulnerabilities and misconfiguration, and Gitleaks for exposed secrets.
Gitleaks checks both Git history and the current worktree. Recognized scanner
policy/ignore inputs are disclosed because they can suppress results.
`--scope all` combines both views.

The selected `--fail-on` level controls only the process exit status. Any
applicable CISA KEV match also exits 1 unless `--fail-on none` is selected. A
lower-severity item may still be urgent when it is internet-facing or in KEV,
and a high CVSS score may be irrelevant when the vulnerable component is not
present or reachable.

`--timeout` limits each native probe or scanner run; a timeout is reported as
incomplete coverage, never as a pass. An explicitly selected missing scanner,
no usable scanner in `auto`, or failed/timed-out/invalid installed scanner
coverage exits 2.

`--output FILE` atomically writes the complete redacted JSON report with
owner-only mode where the platform supports it. `--json` emits the same report
to standard output.

### `doctor`

Checks only the selected scope without performing a full scan. Project/all
checks target safety, scanner executables and versions, and KEV readability;
system/all checks native-platform support. Missing optional scanners are
reported with install guidance.

## Direct CLI

```sh
security-audit audit --scope all --target .
security-audit audit --scope project --target . --scanner auto
security-audit audit --scope project --target . --scanner osv --fail-on high
security-audit doctor --scope project --refresh-kev
security-audit audit --scope project --scanner gitleaks --target . --json
security-audit audit --scope all --target . --output ./security-report.json
security-audit doctor --scope all --target . --kev-catalog ./kev.json
```

Filesystem-root and home-directory targets are refused by default because they
are slow and can cross unrelated trust boundaries. Use `--force` only after
reviewing the target. Scanner processes receive the selected local path;
review each scanner's privacy and data-source behavior before using it on
sensitive code.

See `docs/security-research.md` for the identifier model, official sources,
platform coverage, prioritization method, and known blind spots.
